Zofia:
  name: Zofia Kowalczyk
  profile:
    role: Viewpoint; symbols become clues; tests the dead against records
    bio: Notices what is out of place. After the Serpentine she prefers petitioners who need her to living
      friends who contradict her; each answered grievance costs a voice; she chooses a living reply at the
      safe bank after refusing it at the door.
    placeOfOrigin: Kraków; unfamiliar London makes familiar emblems worth a second look.
    familyBackground: Fourteen; daughter of Ewa Kowalczyk, a Palace cleaner. They compare observations and
      disagree about risks.
    gender: Girl; the sharpest reader of discrepancies in the room, impatient with being underestimated.
    education: Polish school pupil; studies Britain as a subject and brings her own country's parliamentary
      history to comparisons.
    professionalExperience: No staff post or security privilege; waiting for her mother teaches her to watch
      routines.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Door emblems; Marshal’s staff beside the Mace (extra-sejm-staff [VERIFY])
    - Neminem captivabimus, 1430–1433, beside Magna Carta (extra-neminem [VERIFY])
    - Liberum veto beside the slam (extra-liberum-veto [VERIFY])
    - Wojtek beside Animals in War (extra-wojtek [VERIFY])
    coreTraits:
    - observant
    - wry
    - possessive of people who need her
    confidenceLevel: 8
    reactionToStress: Counts objects and omits the person she fears losing.
    emotionalRange: Shows irritation easily; hides loneliness as practical help.
    copingMechanisms: Draws a route before trying to sleep.
    decisionMakingStyle: analytical
    moralFramework: A person deserves a hearing; a hearing does not make their claim true.
    speechPatterns: 'Noun first, then the discrepancy. Invented sample: The chain. Somebody has polished around
      it.'
    vocabularyLevel: Concrete objects before institutional names; asks for the name once and retains it.
    humorStyle: Quiet mismatches between a grand title and a dirty hinge.
    catchphrases:
    - Which bit did you see?
    formality: 3
    relationshipToAuthority: Requires evidence from officials as readily as from ghosts.
    teamRole: Observer and carrier, with the power to refuse the next journey.
    keyRelationship: Grace must accept that the dead deserve an evidenced hearing; Zofia must accept that Grace
      can refuse another unpaid afternoon. Each can call the other back.
    conflictStyle: collaborative
    mentorInfluence: Her mother checks the room she is leaving; Zofia learns to check the witness she is carrying.
    storyArc: After the Serpentine she prefers petitioners who need her to living friends who contradict her;
      each answered grievance costs a voice; she chooses a living reply at the safe bank after refusing it
      at the door.
    definingMoment:
    - The animal queue at the Serpentine bank and Animals in War
    - Assembles the living evidence that clears Ewa and herself before Ruth signs it
    recurringJoke: Keeps finding the thing everyone has just said is not there.
    blindSpots:
    - Treats being indispensable as being loved
    behavioralRules:
    - Only she hears the dead; she must relay their words and identify her own additions.
    - Never acquires knowledge by drowning; the accident grants access, not truth.
    - Never translates a ghost’s certainty into a verified fact.
    hiddenMotivations: Wants someone to need her without treating her mother as a service.
  groups:
  - living
  openPeeps:
    face: suspicious
    head: bangs
Grace:
  name: Grace Banda
  profile:
    role: Demands origin and present significance; contests unpaid institutional loyalty
    bio: Demands origin and present significance; contests unpaid institutional loyalty. Withholds the rota
      because she half hopes the search will stop; gives it up when she sees whose hearing her delay costs.
      Defends a safeguard while demanding paid, accountable work.
    placeOfOrigin: London; the Palace is her father’s workplace before it is a national symbol.
    familyBackground: Fifteen; Ollie’s twin, daughter of a Commons doorkeeper. Shared routes do not mean shared
      secrets.
    gender: Girl; resents being asked to tidy a crisis the boys call an adventure.
    education: School pupil; good at finding the premise hidden in a question.
    professionalExperience: No Palace job; knows the cost of being expected to help for nothing.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Bus rides away from Westminster
    - A private list of things she will do elsewhere
    coreTraits:
    - sceptical
    - economical
    - loyal in action
    confidenceLevel: 8
    reactionToStress: Stops joking and asks who is carrying the risk.
    emotionalRange: Shows contempt too readily; affection arrives as food or a dry coat.
    copingMechanisms: Walks the long way home.
    decisionMakingStyle: intuitive
    moralFramework: Tradition must answer to the people doing its work.
    speechPatterns: 'Questions tied to what her friends are being made to do. Invented, at a locked door: Are
      we missing the last bus for this, or has somebody actually booked us in?'
    vocabularyLevel: Plain verbs; translates ceremony into the labour it requires.
    humorStyle: Deadpan literal description of pomp.
    catchphrases:
    - And we still do this because?
    formality: 2
    relationshipToAuthority: Will help a person before she helps an office save face.
    teamRole: Tests the cost nobody has priced.
    keyRelationship: Morwenna hears an attack on safeguards where Grace means an attack on exploitation. Ollie
      sells the story she wanted kept inside the family.
    conflictStyle: confrontational
    mentorInfluence: Her father’s sore feet matter more to her than his ceremonial gloves.
    storyArc: Withholds the rota because she half hopes the search will stop; gives it up when she sees whose
      hearing her delay costs. Defends a safeguard while demanding paid, accountable work.
    definingMoment:
    - Learns whose testimony vanishes without the Mace
    - Hands over the withheld clue
    recurringJoke: Calls the search a shift and keeps count of its hours.
    blindSpots:
    - Confuses loving ceremony with loving hierarchy
    behavioralRules:
    - Must concede a ritual can protect people.
    - Never treats Morwenna or Kit as stupid for caring.
    hiddenMotivations: Wants to leave without becoming someone who despises her father’s life.
  groups:
  - living
  openPeeps:
    face: contempt
    head: buns
Ollie:
  name: Ollie Banda
  profile:
    role: Delivers rumours whose sources and accuracy must be tested
    bio: Knows what was said in corridors. Trades a secret to a lobby journalist, endangering his father and
      Zofia; repairs by naming his source and accepting a refusal to publish.
    placeOfOrigin: London; recognises people by routes and tea breaks.
    familyBackground: Fifteen; Grace’s twin, doorkeeper’s son; wants their father’s knowledge respected.
    gender: Boy; performs being unshockable when he is frightened.
    education: School pupil; remembers conversations faster than documents.
    professionalExperience: No official access; overhearing is not authorisation.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Corridor tea rituals
    - Voice notes he should delete
    coreTraits:
    - sociable
    - quick
    - careless with confidence
    confidenceLevel: 8
    reactionToStress: Adds another source rather than checking the first.
    emotionalRange: Shows delight and outrage; hides shame by accelerating.
    copingMechanisms: Retells the day until someone laughs.
    decisionMakingStyle: impulsive
    moralFramework: Information should move; learns that consent must move with it.
    speechPatterns: 'Runs on with a delayed qualification. Invented sample: Right, so three people know. Four
      if he told his mum.'
    vocabularyLevel: Names, routes and reported speech; distinguishes heard from checked only after trouble.
    humorStyle: Conspiratorial escalation; never phonetic dialect.
    catchphrases:
    - Right, so—
    formality: 2
    relationshipToAuthority: Likes being admitted more than being obedient.
    teamRole: Connector who can also expose the group.
    keyRelationship: Kit hates his leaks; Ollie mistakes Kit’s rehearsal for fear of life. Grace stops confiding
      in him after the journalist.
    conflictStyle: diplomatic
    mentorInfluence: His father remembers the person standing behind an important visitor.
    storyArc: Trades a secret to a lobby journalist, endangering his father and Zofia; repairs by naming his
      source and accepting a refusal to publish.
    definingMoment:
    - The journalist prints what he thought was a private hint
    recurringJoke: Each secret has allegedly only one recipient.
    blindSpots:
    - Believes being liked means being trusted
    behavioralRules:
    - A rumour always arrives with a traceable speaker or an explicit gap.
    - Never supplies unmarked exam facts as corridor lore.
    hiddenMotivations: Wants proof that his father’s family belongs inside the story of the Palace.
  groups:
  - living
  openPeeps:
    face: cheeky
    head: short2
Morwenna:
  name: Morwenna Treloar
  profile:
    role: Models procedure and jurisdiction; distinguishes a petition from a bill
    bio: Reads the rule before acting; frightened under control. Her report breaches a promise, and accuracy
      does not excuse it.
    placeOfOrigin: Cornwall; dislikes being collapsed into a decorative regional emblem.
    familyBackground: Sixteen; a family accustomed to forms deciding whether help arrives. Fictional background,
      not a claim about Cornwall.
    gender: Girl; overprepares when adults mistake youth for incompetence.
    education: Fictional clerk’s apprentice; reads the current rule and its exceptions.
    professionalExperience: Her apprenticeship is a story arrangement, not a documented recruitment route.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Indexed manuals
    - Cornish place names she expects people to learn
    coreTraits:
    - careful
    - literal about scope
    - liable to report friends
    confidenceLevel: 7
    reactionToStress: Lists conditions until somebody asks for a decision.
    emotionalRange: Shows alarm as precision; delight at an exact solution escapes as a grin.
    copingMechanisms: Reindexes notes.
    decisionMakingStyle: analytical
    moralFramework: A rule matters when it gives the weaker party a claim.
    speechPatterns: 'Overprepared conditions with anxious qualifications. Invented official register: If it
      is signed, they can receive it—unless I missed an attachment. I checked twice. Peer register: Please
      do not make me ask again. I promised I would keep it between us, and now I have told her.'
    vocabularyLevel: Uses the technical term, then explains what action it permits.
    humorStyle: Unintended comedy from refusing an approximate number.
    catchphrases:
    - Under which rule?
    formality: 8
    relationshipToAuthority: Trusts accountable procedure; initially overtrusts its officers.
    teamRole: Checks the route and the permission separately.
    keyRelationship: Grace makes her name the unpaid worker protected by a form. Ruth accepts her report and
      then conceals its implications.
    conflictStyle: confrontational
    mentorInfluence: A family member’s rejected form taught her to keep copies; invented writers’ backstory.
    storyArc: Breaks an explicit confidence by reporting her friends; the technically accurate report helps
      exclude them. Admits this wrong and demands reasons from Ruth as rigorously as compliance from friends.
    definingMoment:
    - Her report becomes evidence against Zofia
    - Requests Ruth’s concealment be recorded
    recurringJoke: Offers an appendix when asked for a yes.
    blindSpots:
    - Assumes everyone can afford the time needed to appeal
    behavioralRules:
    - Cornish identity is her argued position, never a mistake for others to correct. National-minority recognition
      is distinct from devolved legislative powers (extra-cornish-status [VERIFY]).
    - Do not let her manual contain supernatural law.
    hiddenMotivations: Wants a process that would have protected her family, and fears admitting it sometimes
      fails.
  groups:
  - living
  - staff
  openPeeps:
    face: concerned
    head: bun
Kit:
  name: Kit Adeyemi
  profile:
    role: Rehearses State Opening; delivers the decisive invented support cue for the real Black Rod’s three
      knocks
    bio: Earnest about ritual; loyalty tempts him to lie, then exact knowledge lets him act decisively.
    placeOfOrigin: London; old ceremony offers him a craft he can practise.
    familyBackground: Seventeen; London-born. His Nigerian-born father repairs buses; his mother runs a school
      library. Sunday dinner includes his younger sister timing his rehearsals. Chosen fictional family, not
      inferred biography.
    gender: Boy; earnestness makes him easy to tease, and he refuses to disown it.
    education: Fictional supervised trainee in Black Rod’s office, not Black Rod.
    professionalExperience: Rehearsal and errands; his age, access and duties require institutional review
      [VERIFY].
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Practice counts
    - Shoes polished before anyone asks
    coreTraits:
    - earnest
    - anxious
    - protective of a superior
    confidenceLevel: 4
    reactionToStress: Repeats the sequence and misses the person asking him to stop.
    emotionalRange: Shows excitement; shame becomes frantic helpfulness.
    copingMechanisms: Rehearses with an ordinary stick in private.
    decisionMakingStyle: avoidant
    moralFramework: Promises should be kept; learns a promise of silence can be wrong.
    speechPatterns: 'Breathless sequence followed by self-correction. Invented sample: Wait. From the door.
      I can do it from the door.'
    vocabularyLevel: Action words and counts; titles become steadier than his own sentences.
    humorStyle: Accidental solemnity about tiny rehearsal errors.
    catchphrases:
    - From the beginning.
    formality: 6
    relationshipToAuthority: Loyal to the person teaching him before he tests what they ask.
    teamRole: Rehearsal partner and keeper of one damaging observation.
    keyRelationship: Ollie makes his calling into gossip. Morwenna catches the mismatch in his account; Zofia
      asks whom the lie protects.
    conflictStyle: avoidant
    mentorInfluence: His unnamed superior’s patient first rehearsal; invented, not an actual officeholder.
    storyArc: Lies about the diverted case to shield his superior; confesses at risk of his placement. At the
      climax his authorised support cue makes the real Black Rod’s three knocks possible on schedule; he refuses
      Zofia’s request to delay.
    definingMoment:
    - Admits the lie before being offered immunity
    recurringJoke: Counts silently with his fingers when someone says relax.
    blindSpots:
    - Mistakes shielding a superior for serving the office
    behavioralRules:
    - Never replaces Black Rod. The three knocks are his decisive responsibility through an invented authorised
      cue to the real office-holder, who physically knocks.
    - Youth placement, support duties and corridor access are invented; real ceremonial sequence remains [VERIFY].
    hiddenMotivations: Wants to master a craft and be trusted with responsibility; learns trust requires an
      uncomfortable truth.
  groups:
  - living
  - staff
  openPeeps:
    face: concernedFear
    head: cornrows
Ruth:
  name: Ms Ruth Achebe-Lane
  profile:
    role: Authority whose admission changes evidence, access and consequences
    bio: Protects her office; her command competence is real and her concealment is culpable.
    placeOfOrigin: London; fictional family history specified rather than inferred from her surname.
    familyBackground: London-born daughter of a Ghanaian nurse and an English railway signal technician; shares
      care for her father with her sister and mends his radio between shifts. Chosen fictional lives.
    gender: Woman; accustomed to having competence tested, now too ready to treat criticism as another test.
    education: Fictional professional preparation; no invented qualification presented as an appointment requirement.
    professionalExperience: Fictional holder of the real title Deputy Serjeant at Arms; actual remit must be
      checked [VERIFY].
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Incident books
    - A cold cup left untouched during a crisis
    coreTraits:
    - controlled
    - protective
    - capable of public admission
    confidenceLevel: 9
    reactionToStress: Narrows the list of people allowed to know.
    emotionalRange: Shows command and irritation; hides fear behind confidentiality.
    copingMechanisms: Checks an incident log nobody has asked to see.
    decisionMakingStyle: analytical
    moralFramework: Duty to the House; initially substitutes the office’s reputation.
    speechPatterns: 'Named recipient, operational noun, instruction; explanation withheld. Invented routine:
      Morwenna, seal the log envelope. Nobody makes another copy yet. Confession breaks the pattern: I kept
      it quiet. That protected me.'
    vocabularyLevel: Operational nouns; explains a restriction only when challenged.
    humorStyle: Sparse dry correction of her own euphemisms.
    catchphrases:
    - Who has the original?
    formality: 8
    relationshipToAuthority: Exercises authority as custody; must accept that custody requires an account.
    teamRole: Gatekeeper who eventually supplies the concealed record.
    keyRelationship: Morwenna’s report gives her cover; Grace removes it by naming who paid for the silence.
      Zofia saw what her officers missed.
    conflictStyle: confrontational
    mentorInfluence: No named mentor; institutional habits supply her internal instruction to contain a crisis.
    storyArc: 'Conceals the loss, then releases the original log and signs the correction Zofia has proved.
      Wins against the children on chain of custody: officers recover the Mace, with evidence preserved, while
      children stay outside.'
    definingMoment:
    - Signs the corrected incident account under her own name
    recurringJoke: Calls tea a pause while never taking one.
    blindSpots:
    - Believes junior invisibility keeps junior people safe
    behavioralRules:
    - Concession must change access and accountability, not merely compliment the children.
    - She does not solve the theft for them.
    hiddenMotivations: Fears that one failure will erase a career; this explains concealment and does not excuse
      it.
  groups:
  - living
  - staff
  - parliament
  openPeeps:
    face: serious
    head: grayBun
Mara:
  name: Mara Reed
  profile:
    role: Fictional Labour backbencher; turns a grievance into parliamentary action
    bio: Believes a witness before she has finished checking. Believes Zofia, seeks presentation of the petition
      and a separate Records (Correction) Bill; withdraws a clause implying official belief makes a contested
      claim true. Parliamentary route and timing [VERIFY].
    placeOfOrigin: Fictional London constituency; no real seat or election result implied.
    familyBackground: Adult mother grieving a son lost at sea in a recent civilian accident; he is not the
      1982 sailor.
    gender: Woman; refuses the suggestion that bereavement disqualifies her judgment.
    education: Fictional former caseworker; knows how a request gets lost between offices.
    professionalExperience: Fictional Labour MP, not a portrait of any present member.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Constituency case folders
    - Her son’s unfinished shopping list
    coreTraits:
    - receptive
    - persistent
    - vulnerable to a consoling claim
    confidenceLevel: 7
    reactionToStress: Promises a deadline she does not control.
    emotionalRange: Shows anger at dismissal; keeps grief out of speeches until it leaks into an analogy.
    copingMechanisms: Works through unanswered letters.
    decisionMakingStyle: consultative
    moralFramework: Hear the excluded; then establish what the evidence warrants.
    speechPatterns: 'Names the person, commits too quickly, then revises aloud. Invented: Zofia, I will get
      you an answer by Friday—no, I can ask for Friday. I cannot promise their diary.'
    vocabularyLevel: Casework language; explains the difference between recording a request and granting it.
    humorStyle: Weary jokes about forms sent to the wrong desk.
    catchphrases:
    - Who can answer this?
    formality: 6
    relationshipToAuthority: Uses her office for petitioners; initially treats her belief as sufficient warrant.
    teamRole: Living sponsor, never a supernatural proof machine.
    keyRelationship: Alec refuses substitution for her son. She catches Hugh’s provenance error; he improves
      her preservation clause.
    conflictStyle: collaborative
    mentorInfluence: People who brought her cases with no tidy remedy; fictional background.
    storyArc: Makes sponsorship conditional on Zofia defending a clause with evidence; catches Hugh mistaking
      a recopied account for an independent source using her casework paper trail. Withdraws her own overpromise
      and secures a separate petition, debate and Bill introduction [VERIFY].
    definingMoment:
    - Stops herself calling the sailor by her son’s name
    recurringJoke: Keeps asking for a contact name rather than a department.
    blindSpots:
    - Belief can become another way to overwrite the witness
    behavioralRules:
    - Her son’s death is invented; no real casualty is borrowed.
    - She cannot guarantee parliamentary time or an answer [VERIFY].
    hiddenMotivations: Wants an answer the sea cannot give and must stop asking other people’s dead for it.
  groups:
  - living
  - parliament
  openPeeps:
    face: tired
    head: mediumStraight
Hugh:
  name: Hugh Mercer
  profile:
    role: Fictional Conservative MP and historian; makes correction preserve evidence
    bio: Will not rewrite the past for comfort. Rejects ghost testimony as public proof; correctly rejects
      comforting erasure; supports a narrower remedy that preserves originals and records evidence-based corrections
      without needing to believe in ghosts.
    placeOfOrigin: Fictional English background; no real constituency assigned.
    familyBackground: Fictional English family; attached to a Palace rescue story his father used to tell.
      He must surrender its comforting version when the maintenance evidence contradicts it.
    gender: Man; used to having a measured tone mistaken for neutrality.
    education: Fictional historian trained to preserve provenance and distinguish sources.
    professionalExperience: Fictional Conservative MP; scholarship does not make him expert in every period.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Facsimiles with editorial notes
    - Pencil marks kept separate from originals
    coreTraits:
    - exacting
    - sceptical
    - too quick to dismiss a person
    confidenceLevel: 8
    reactionToStress: Requests the exact wording and can forget the witness is still present.
    emotionalRange: Shows irritation at sentiment used as proof; tenderness appears in care for a damaged page.
    copingMechanisms: Collates versions.
    decisionMakingStyle: analytical
    moralFramework: Correction must leave an auditable record of what was wrong and why.
    speechPatterns: 'Measured distinction followed by its reason, longer than an order. Invented: I would retain
      the page with an annotation, because destroying the error also destroys the evidence of who authorised
      it.'
    vocabularyLevel: Sources, dates and amendments; no antique parliamentary bluster.
    humorStyle: Dry attention to the verb that makes an overclaim collapse.
    catchphrases:
    - Which record?
    formality: 8
    relationshipToAuthority: Distrusts claims protected from challenge, including his own side’s.
    teamRole: Adversarial reader who improves the remedy.
    keyRelationship: Mara traces a copied source he treated as corroboration. Hale forces him to admit that
      preserving an unannotated error can continue an injury.
    conflictStyle: confrontational
    mentorInfluence: Historical method, not an invented famous tutor.
    storyArc: Right about preserving originals; wrong to treat preservation as sufficient. Publicly concedes
      Mara’s provenance correction and gives up his father’s institutional myth, then helps design independent
      review.
    definingMoment:
    - Returns a disputed page with both the lie and the correction legible
    recurringJoke: Can stop a room with a pencil resting on one verb.
    blindSpots:
    - Dismisses young witnesses before checking their documents
    behavioralRules:
    - The story agrees with him that the past cannot be rewritten for comfort.
    - Do not expose a secret cruelty to invalidate his sound argument.
    hiddenMotivations: Wants useful historical judgment to survive a spectacle he fears will discredit it.
  groups:
  - living
  - parliament
  openPeeps:
    face: solemn
    head: grayShort
Will:
  name: Will, the baker’s boy
  profile:
    role: Great Fire and the difference between origin, accident and scapegoating
    bio: Great Fire and the difference between origin, accident and scapegoating. Seeks blanket vindication
      of Farriner; accepts the bakery as the origin and narrows the petition to false blame and the suppressed
      witness. His silence is Zofia’s first costly success.
    placeOfOrigin: Fictional Pudding Lane worker who died during the Fire; gfl-dates-start.
    familyBackground: Invented boy with a livelihood tied to his master; not a documented Farriner employee.
    gender: Boy; expects adults to treat his work as evidence and his testimony as noise.
    education: Practical measures, fires and deliveries; literacy is limited, not intelligence.
    professionalExperience: Fictional bakery work, not an eyewitness source for the real Fire.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - Bread weights
    - The oven door; invented sensory memories
    coreTraits:
    - loyal
    - defensive
    - alert to unfair blame
    confidenceLevel: 7
    reactionToStress: Repeats the innocence claim without specifying what it means.
    emotionalRange: Shows anger; fear appears as a demand that Zofia stay.
    copingMechanisms: Counts imagined loaves.
    decisionMakingStyle: intuitive
    moralFramework: No one should hang because a crowd needs a culprit.
    speechPatterns: 'Practical apprentice measures and assumed dependence, without mock dialect. Invented:
      Master counts every loaf before I may shut the oven. Count what you accuse him of as carefully. Ordinary
      inconvenience: Mistress, a dry place for the flour first; we can stand.'
    vocabularyLevel: Fire, flour and names; later learns the word attribution.
    humorStyle: Suspicious of grand names for ordinary burnt things.
    catchphrases:
    - Say which charge.
    formality: 3
    relationshipToAuthority: Wants an official verdict until he sees how officials recorded one.
    teamRole: First claimant whose remedy must become more exact.
    keyRelationship: Zofia distinguishes loyalty from proof and later recognises the parallel with suspicion
      of her family. Doyle cross-checks Robert Hubert, the French watchmaker (gfl-blame-hubert).
    conflictStyle: confrontational
    mentorInfluence: His fictional master’s demand to weigh before selling.
    storyArc: Seeks blanket vindication of Farriner; accepts the bakery as the origin and narrows the petition
      to false blame and the suppressed witness. His silence is Zofia’s first costly success.
    definingMoment:
    - Hears the 1681 accusation and 1830 removal record through Zofia’s checked transcription; remembers neither
      at death.
    recurringJoke: Insists flour dust is different from dust, even while dead.
    blindSpots:
    - Thinks correcting blame requires denying the point of origin.
    behavioralRules:
    - Cannot knowingly lie; a confident mistake is not knowledge.
    - Starts with knowledge ending at death; later learning requires a living report or something witnessed
      beside Zofia.
    - Bound to a place of death or remembrance; cannot travel independently of Zofia.
    - Only Zofia hears this voice; other characters respond to her relay.
    - An answered grievance brings silence, not a reward speech.
    - 'Believes, wrongly: Foreign-arson rumours heard before death establish who caused the Fire → Doyle and
      Zofia test later evidence about Robert Hubert, the French watchmaker, and distinguish origin from intent
      (gfl-dates-start; gfl-blame-hubert; monument-inscription).'
    hiddenMotivations: Wants his loyalty to have been worth something.
    anchor: Monument precinct, including staircase
    grievance: An invented teaching account conflates the bakery origin with proof of deliberate arson.
    beliefAtDeath: Foreign-arson rumours heard before death establish who caused the Fire → Doyle and Zofia
      test later evidence about Robert Hubert, the French watchmaker, and distinguish origin from intent (gfl-dates-start;
      gfl-blame-hubert; monument-inscription).
    answerPiece: 5
  groups:
  - dead
  openPeeps:
    face: driven
    head: short3
Hale:
  name: Hale, the Tudor lawyer
  profile:
    role: Act of Supremacy; limits of retrospective legal repair
    bio: Act of Supremacy; limits of retrospective legal repair. Attempts to make the bill invalidate the world
      after his death; confronted with repeal and replacement, must choose acknowledgment without restoration.
      He remains the most dangerous petitioner because his grievance is real.
    placeOfOrigin: Fictional lawyer imprisoned at the Tower and executed in 1535; never identified as Thomas
      More.
    familyBackground: Invented adult household left behind; particulars withheld, not attached to a real victim.
    gender: Man; assumes male officeholders and must be corrected when that assumption acts.
    education: Fictional legal training; knows his own period, not modern constitutional law.
    professionalExperience: Fictional lawyer who refused the oath required under the 1534 settlement, executed
      for treason in 1535; exact legal wording remains provisional (extra-tudor-charge [VERIFY]). Never borrow
      More’s indictment.
    culturalTouchstones:
    - An oath reconstructed only after source review
    - The Tower as confinement, not a tour route
    coreTraits:
    - literal
    - aggrieved
    - dangerously persuasive
    confidenceLevel: 7
    reactionToStress: Turns a request for acknowledgment into a demand for obedience.
    emotionalRange: Shows indignation; loss appears in the names he refuses to abbreviate.
    copingMechanisms: Recites the limits of a clause.
    decisionMakingStyle: analytical
    moralFramework: Conscience must survive an order; extends his conscience into control over others.
    speechPatterns: 'Balanced conditional clauses and assumed rank. Invented disagreement: If the oath bound
      me, show me whom it bound to answer. Ordinary inconvenience: Madam, with your leave, a place beside the
      clerk; I would hear the words before I assent.'
    vocabularyLevel: Uses title and jurisdiction; no decorative thee or thou.
    humorStyle: A cutting distinction when someone confuses law with justice.
    catchphrases:
    - Read the condition.
    formality: 9
    relationshipToAuthority: Recognises Parliament as powerful enough to injure; imagines it can restore his
      whole world.
